# APTR-11. A real environment file is a runtime materialization of the vault, never a source
# artifact — so nothing that could hold one is committable. The example is the single exception,
# and it is gated: `npm --prefix client run assert-config-documented` fails the build on any line
# in it that assigns a value.
#
# The allowance is one exact PATH, not a filename and not a pattern:
#
#   * `.env.example.local` and `.env.example.prod` stay ignored, because an allowance that
#     matched a family would have re-opened the hole the rule above it exists to close.
#   * the leading `/` anchors it to the repository root. Without it Git applies the negation to
#     that BASENAME at any depth, so `client/.env.example` — a file the documentation gate never
#     reads, because it only ever opens the root one — would have been committable carrying real
#     values. The gate and the allowance now name the same single file.
.env
.env.*
!/.env.example
